{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/0477864853171cf0afdcc87e2debb27c24efa8b6166cc396d8f7cd0a126f7ace.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/a6e3e898672afd315ae56af8a6e098565947b3cf8c7c2e86792e27e8e8775cdb.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/87b1cb524aee7db1f7e6f2a34c81874d2f9dae66d94a4e7b8b5c63b3713d9da7.png)" 13194139535511 true] [17592186133575 1100 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T$, extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535511 false] [17592186133575 1100 "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/969339570bc776ebf69a7f6e9cbff0377fa35842a913e68680d8bd2ed0a1decb.png)" 13194139535511 true] [17592186133575 1145 "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2$. To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$, we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\ &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{u_{1} \\cdot x = 0; u_{2} \\cdot x = 0\n$$\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}:=\\sqrt{2} \\cdot R_{1} \\\\ R_{2}:=\\sqrt{6} \\cdot R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3$, and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3$.\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{3} \\\\ -x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting $x_3=1$, we obtain the vector\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535511 false] [17592186133575 1145 "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/e7034bdd15c1adc9aca80284bb2ab9b5a99ce5f33fa65f7b867c542158727b9d.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  