}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-3{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352982 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352983 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352984 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352985 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352986 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1041 "selection" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352983 13194139535511 true] [17592186352981 1110 #uuid "ab297600-23e7-543f-a2eb-aff4fd05fe86" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352982 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352982 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535511 true] [17592186352982 1111 #uuid "5fdabf14-a362-5508-af3f-adb65ea0ceae" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352983 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352983 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535511 true] [17592186352983 1111 #uuid "edd2c9ce-cbcd-5d2a-98aa-bccb9c2dc6f8" 13194139535511 true] [17592186352984 1034 "3" 13194139535511 true] [17